  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a genetic algorithm framework to explore diverse configurations of step functions that could potentially yield a lower C_5 value than the initial parent solution. The algorithm will maintain a population of binary vectors, each representing a candidate step function, and evolve them through crossover and mutation operators. We incorporate repair mechanisms to ensure all candidates satisfy the constraints (binary [0,1] values and sum=200). The rationale is that the parent solution may be stuck in a local optimum, and a population-based search with diverse solutions and repair operations will discover new areas of the solution space that could yield improvements.\n\nThe approach addresses several limitations of the parent algorithm:\n- The parent relies heavily on gradient-based methods identified by thresholding, which may miss certain features\n- It doesn't systematically explore diverse solution topologies\n- It primarily uses local optimization, potentially missing better global optima\n\nThe mathematical foundation comes from treating the step function as a binary optimization problem with Hamming distance as the distance measure. This approach is suitable because the optimal function is expected to be Boolean-valued, and the non-vectorized implementation in the parent code is handled automatically by discrete averaging.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: Binary vectors of length n=400 (positions) with exactly 200 ones (representing the step function support)\n- Objective: Minimize C_5(h) = max_{k} \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx, approximated by the discretized version using full correlation\n- Constraints:\n  - h(x) \u2208 {0,1} \n  - \u2211h_i = 200 (approximately 0.5 total integral)\n- Operations:\n  - Mutation: Bit flipping with repair\n  - Crossover: One-point crossover\n- Fitness Evaluation:\n  - Current population follows a truncated normal distribution with mean 0.5 and variance 0.2\n  - Use decision heuristic Tmax(max_value - best_value) as stopping criterion\n- Repair Mechanism:\n  - Maintain feasibility by preserving a fixed number of ones\n  - Adjust non-feasible individuals by flipping and repairing\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe algorithm will begin by:\n1. Converting the parent solution to binary representation with exactly 200 ones\n2. Generating an initial population of 50 candidates by flipping bits while preserving feasibility\n3. Evolving the population through CPLEX IPM-based crossover and repair operators\n4. Evaluating objectives in parallel using OpenMP\n5. Maintaining a cache of the best individual found during evolution\n6. Monitoring runtime using a distributed wall-clock timer\n\nGenetic algorithm operations:\n- Tournament selection with size 4\n- Configurable crossover and mutation probabilities (cx_prob=0.5, mut_prob=0.1)\n- Repair mutation triggered by declining fitness over 10 generations\n- Elitism with the best individual preserved across generations\n\nThe algorithm will terminate when:\n- Tmax iterations (75 to 150) are completed\n- Significant performance degradation is observed\n- Wall-clock time exceeds 1300 seconds\n\n4. Search choices and use cases\nThe algorithm is configured with:\n- Primary crossover mode (ONE)\n- Maximum population size 50\n- Repair mutation rate 1.0\n- CPLEX parameter settings optimized for middle-sized problems\n- Migration settings to periodically sample 2 new random solutions\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nThe dominant cost is approximately 50 * 10 * 400 * (time for C_5 evaluation) operations per time unit. Performance monitoring checks occur periodically to control execution.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nCandidates are validated using:\n- Binary repair indicators\n- Objective value recording\n- CPLEX solution accept/cycle lock mechanisms\n- Post-mortem analysis of champion dispersion metrics\n\nFinal solutions undergo rigorous feasibility checks and multiple restart phases.\n\nFinesse IPM crossover and repair constraints are enforced using:\n- Mutation repair techniques maintaining exact intervals\n- Repair priority by status and grade\n- Steady-state insertion control",
